Scoutmastership in seven minutes or less.

51
00:03:42,000 --> 00:03:46,000
Part of Scoutmastership is continually reassessing first principles,

52
00:03:46,000 --> 00:03:52,000
And that maintenance of our focus and direction requires some inspiration.

53
00:03:52,000 --> 00:04:01,000
Now I found on Google Books a facsimile of the 1913 proof edition of the handbook for Scoutmasters.

54
00:04:01,000 --> 00:04:09,000
This was the draft that was distributed for comment to perfect the final first edition of the Scoutmaster handbook,

55
00:04:09,000 --> 00:04:14,000
And I was looking through it and I thought that they did some things just wonderfully.

56
00:04:14,000 --> 00:04:17,000
I mean, they worded some pieces just eloquently,

57
00:04:17,000 --> 00:04:29,000
And although it was written almost 100 years ago, it was the basis for the first edition of the Scoutmaster handbook.

58
00:04:29,000 --> 00:04:31,000
These things haven't changed,

59
00:04:31,000 --> 00:04:33,000
They're still the same,

60
00:04:33,000 --> 00:04:37,000
And so I thought I would share a couple of excerpts from it with you,

61
00:04:37,000 --> 00:04:47,000
Just a way to inspire you to maintain your focus and to get direction from these broad underlying principles that have remained constant through a century of scouting.

62
00:04:47,000 --> 00:04:52,000
Here's a piece written about the intentions of scouting:

63
00:04:52,000 --> 00:05:03,000
The Scout movement is not antagonistic to any civic enterprise, but rather seeks to cooperate with all other good movements in the interest of the boy.

64
00:05:04,000 --> 00:05:10,000
The movement is wholly non-sectarian and plans to work with every sect and creed alike.

65
00:05:10,000 --> 00:05:19,000
It is non-military and seeks to promote peace scouting and to develop educational character building for good citizenship.

66
00:05:19,000 --> 00:05:21,000
It is wholly non-partisan.

67
00:05:21,000 --> 00:05:31,000
It cannot favor one against another and cannot countenance interference on any debatable questions, whether social, religious or political.

68
00:05:31,000 --> 00:05:43,000
It seeks to make the boy a more useful and appreciative son to his parents or those to whom he owes his home comforts, a more diligent and obedient student in his school life,

69
00:05:43,000 --> 00:05:49,000
a more valuable aid to the community in promoting its material progress and protection,

70
00:05:49,000 --> 00:06:00,000
and a more efficient and better prepared young man in development for future citizenship and the pleasures and hardships of mature existence.

71
00:06:00,000 --> 00:06:08,000
I think that's just an incredibly eloquent statement and a fantastic encapsulation of the intent behind scouting:

72
00:06:08,000 --> 00:06:13,000
Not to be sectarian, not to be political, not to be military,

73
00:06:13,000 --> 00:06:25,000
To cooperate with all the good influences within a community, to help young men grow and build character, to be a contributing part of that community.

74
00:06:25,000 --> 00:06:28,000
They had this to say about discipline:

75
00:06:28,000 --> 00:06:38,000
A scoutmaster should not be petty in his discipline but should stand on the high plane of honor in everything.

76
00:06:38,000 --> 00:06:47,000
On the other hand, he should be careful not to be lax and the boy scout should understand that when he has said a thing he means it.

77
00:06:47,000 --> 00:06:57,000
Liberal and cautious in his judgment, but firm in his attitude when once his judgment has been made, should be the rule of the scoutmaster in matters of discipline.

78
00:06:57,000 --> 00:07:06,000
I like the fact that the original draft edition of the handbook said: don't be petty,

79
00:07:06,000 --> 00:07:13,000
Don't sweat the small stuff- I think would be an appropriate modern translation of that-

80
00:07:13,000 --> 00:07:17,000
But stand on the high plane of honor in everything and when you say something, mean it.

81
00:07:17,000 --> 00:07:24,000
I mean this is good general advice for all parts of our lives, not just being a scoutmaster.

82
00:07:24,000 --> 00:07:28,000
Here's what the draft handbook had to say about reverence.

83
00:07:28,000 --> 00:07:33,000
Law 12 directs the attention of the boy to the idea of reverence.

84
00:07:33,000 --> 00:07:48,000
It holds his religious duties as an ideal worthy of being worked for and urges upon him the duty and responsibility of respecting the convictions of every other man in matters of tradition, custom and religion.

85
00:07:48,000 --> 00:07:58,000
Should he think that he has more light on these matters than the man next to him, he should be the more generous and liberal because of that fact.

86
00:07:58,000 --> 00:08:15,000
Now again, very well worded, very eloquent. but when it talks about reverence, it talks about reverence that is very inclusive and very accepting of tradition, custom and religion.

87
00:08:15,000 --> 00:08:37,000
Then it goes on to say: if you think you've got it, if you think you have a corner on the truth, that should really allow you to be more generous, because you know that the way that you feel about your particular way of life and the particular way that you do things is felt as strongly by that other guy, that other person.

88
00:08:37,000 --> 00:08:42,000
I just think that's an outstanding little study on reverence,

89
00:08:42,000 --> 00:08:56,000
On dynamic leadership. the draft had this to say: the idea that must continually be kept in mind is the boy's good and the boy rather than scouting.

90
00:08:56,000 --> 00:09:06,000
Isn't that interesting? The idea that must continually be kept in mind is the boy's good and the boy rather than scouting.

91
00:09:06,000 --> 00:09:09,000
I think that's a great point.

92
00:09:09,000 --> 00:09:19,000
Half of our teachers in the public schools, it goes on to say, are trying to teach the subject matter of the book when they ought to be teaching the boy.

93
00:09:19,000 --> 00:09:22,000
They employ static methods.

94
00:09:22,000 --> 00:09:27,000
You can set up a goal for attainment and the boy will reach the goal.

95
00:09:27,000 --> 00:09:31,000
Generally, however, he will go no higher than you point.

96
00:09:31,000 --> 00:09:35,000
Your teaching should be dynamic rather than static.

97
00:09:35,000 --> 00:09:47,000
What I get out of those couple of statements are that what we're trying to do with our scouts is to inspire them, to set goals and then inspire them to meet them, inspire them to pass on them.

98
00:09:47,000 --> 00:09:56,000
But it's this dynamism, it's this dynamic element of scouting that makes it different from every other occupation that the boy is following.

99
00:09:56,000 --> 00:09:58,000
It makes it different from sports,

100
00:09:58,000 --> 00:10:00,000
It makes it different from school.

101
00:10:00,000 --> 00:10:03,000
They might not be able to set the goals as they want them,

102
00:10:03,000 --> 00:10:06,000
They might not be able to follow them as they see fit,

103
00:10:06,000 --> 00:10:19,000
But in scouting they have this kind of experience where they can do stuff according to their own lights, according to what they want to do, according to the way that they see fit,

104
00:10:19,000 --> 00:10:21,000
And they can try stuff out

105
00:10:21,000 --> 00:10:28,000
And they'll succeed. they'll fail, but they'll do it in a safe environment that is encouraging and nurturing.

106
00:10:28,000 --> 00:10:32,000
Now on the ultimate aim of scouting. it says this:

107
00:10:32,000 --> 00:10:36,000
Aim: to secure balanced, symmetrical activities for your patrols.

108
00:10:36,000 --> 00:10:39,000
Remember, your scout is four-sided.

109
00:10:39,000 --> 00:10:43,000
He is physical, mental, social and religious in his nature.

110
00:10:43,000 --> 00:10:50,000
Do not neglect any one side of him, but get the proper agencies to cooperate with you for these ends.

111
00:10:50,000 --> 00:10:54,000
Let the boys do whatever they can.

112
00:10:54,000 --> 00:10:56,000
I'm going to repeat that.

113
00:10:56,000 --> 00:10:59,000
Let the boys do whatever they can.

114
00:10:59,000 --> 00:11:04,000
Merely insist on adequate adult supervision.

115
00:11:04,000 --> 00:11:09,000
Above all, be patient, practical and businesslike.

116
00:11:09,000 --> 00:11:14,000
And remember that old heads never grow on young shoulders.

117
00:11:14,000 --> 00:11:23,000
The scout master should take his place in the community by the side of the teacher of secular and religious instruction.

118
00:11:23,000 --> 00:11:31,000
He is an educator and is dealing with the most plastic and most valuable asset in the community: boyhood.

119
00:11:31,000 --> 00:11:34,000
Let him take his task seriously.

120
00:11:34,000 --> 00:11:38,000
Look upon his privilege with a desire to accomplish great things

121
00:11:38,000 --> 00:11:42,000
And always remember the good of the boys is his ultimate aim.

122
00:11:42,000 --> 00:11:45,000
I'm really interested in that statement.

123
00:11:45,000 --> 00:11:48,000
Let the boys do whatever they can

124
00:11:48,000 --> 00:11:52,000
And merely insist on adequate adult supervision.

125
00:11:52,000 --> 00:11:55,000
I think that's a great way to put it.

126
00:11:55,000 --> 00:11:57,000
You know we talk about boy leadership.

127
00:11:57,000 --> 00:12:00,000
We talk about troops being boy led and everything like that.

128
00:12:00,000 --> 00:12:03,000
Well, you know, define that for me.

129
00:12:03,000 --> 00:12:04,000
There you go.

130
00:12:04,000 --> 00:12:06,000
Let the boy do whatever they can,

131
00:12:06,000 --> 00:12:10,000
Just insist on adequate adult supervision.

132
00:12:10,000 --> 00:12:13,000
Be patient, practical and businesslike,

133
00:12:13,000 --> 00:12:19,000
And remember that old heads never grow on young shoulders.

134
00:12:19,000 --> 00:12:21,000
That's a very good thing to remember.

135
00:12:21,000 --> 00:12:24,000
It will save you a lot of time and a lot of frustration,

136
00:12:24,000 --> 00:12:27,000
And it's stated right in there with an encouragement to be patient,

137
00:12:27,000 --> 00:12:29,000
Because you do need a lot of patience.

138
00:12:29,000 --> 00:12:33,000
Look upon this privilege with a desire to accomplish great things

139
00:12:33,000 --> 00:12:38,000
And always remember the good of the boys is our ultimate aim.

140
00:12:38,000 --> 00:12:45,000
So that's all from the draft 1913 edition of the first edition of the Scoutmaster Handbook.

141
00:12:45,000 --> 00:12:49,000
I'll have a link to it on the blog post that contains this podcast

142
00:12:49,000 --> 00:12:52,000
If you want to go in and look around and read some more about it.

143
00:12:52,000 --> 00:12:58,000
But I thought it was a great inspiration on how we go forward in our role as scout leaders.

