157
00:14:04,000 --> 00:14:09,000
Kind of curious as to some suggestions for integrating these new guys into the troop,

158
00:14:09,000 --> 00:14:13,000
We've talked a little bit about maybe having new scout patrols for a few months so everyone's up to speed

159
00:14:13,000 --> 00:14:16,000
And have some of the other older guys act as troop guides.

160
00:14:16,000 --> 00:14:20,000
We've also talked a little bit about mixing them into our existing patrols,

161
00:14:20,000 --> 00:14:27,000
So our patrols are still kind of fragmenting right now due to age and interest in other things going on.

162
00:14:27,000 --> 00:14:32,000
Hopefully, though, if it had been like just a small handful, we probably could have managed them in

163
00:14:32,000 --> 00:14:38,000
With this big number- at least big for our troop- seems that that's the best way to approach this.

164
00:14:38,000 --> 00:14:40,000
We're a little concerned about that,

165
00:14:40,000 --> 00:14:42,000
So any thoughts you might have would be great.

166
00:14:42,000 --> 00:14:43,000
Thanks,

167
00:14:43,000 --> 00:14:44,000
Talk to you later, Clark.

168
00:14:44,000 --> 00:14:52,000
You can get in touch through the Google voicemail service and you'll hear a little bit about how you can do that towards the end of the podcast.

169
00:14:52,000 --> 00:14:58,000
And Jason is concerned because he has a tidal wave coming to his troop.

170
00:14:58,000 --> 00:15:04,000
So if you got about 30 guys, maybe four or five of them are getting ready to age out.

171
00:15:04,000 --> 00:15:08,000
So now you're looking at it- maybe like 26 or so-

172
00:15:08,000 --> 00:15:13,000
And then you're looking at 16 to 20 new guys coming in.

173
00:15:13,000 --> 00:15:15,000
Oh, that's going to be great.

174
00:15:15,000 --> 00:15:19,000
Oh, it's going to have some challenges and things like that,

175
00:15:19,000 --> 00:15:21,000
But boy, it's going to be great.

176
00:15:21,000 --> 00:15:29,000
And if it's the first time you've managed a giant wave of new guys coming into the troop, first of all, prepare your youth leadership,

177
00:15:29,000 --> 00:15:32,000
And you have been talking to them about this, I'm sure,

178
00:15:32,000 --> 00:15:35,000
And you've been consulting with them about what they would like to do.

179
00:15:35,000 --> 00:15:39,000
Do you think new scout patrol is a good idea, guys?

180
00:15:39,000 --> 00:15:40,000
Is that the way we should go?

181
00:15:40,000 --> 00:15:43,000
Do you want to just incorporate them into new patrols?

182
00:15:43,000 --> 00:15:46,000
Is it time for us to redo all of our patrols?

183
00:15:46,000 --> 00:15:48,000
How do you want to handle it, fellas?

184
00:15:48,000 --> 00:15:53,000
Do you think it's important that we ask the Weeblows coming in as new scouts what they think

185
00:15:53,000 --> 00:15:57,000
and how they would like to do things as far as setting up patrols and stuff?

186
00:15:57,000 --> 00:16:04,000
The question of setting up patrols, I know it seems like a big challenge right now, but it's pretty easy.

187
00:16:04,000 --> 00:16:06,000
I would leave it to your youth leadership.

188
00:16:06,000 --> 00:16:08,000
They're going to have some pretty good answers.

189
00:16:08,000 --> 00:16:14,000
The dynamic of having that many new guys in the troop that might not settle down to be something

190
00:16:14,000 --> 00:16:19,000
comprehensible for a month or two or for the course of a year.

191
00:16:19,000 --> 00:16:26,000
So I would be ready to ride the tidal wave, get your surfboard out, get geared up and get ready to go.

192
00:16:26,000 --> 00:16:29,000
You're going to do this by preparing your youth leaders for it.

193
00:16:29,000 --> 00:16:35,000
I would sit down with them and I would say: okay, let's remind ourselves of exactly what we're doing.

194
00:16:35,000 --> 00:16:41,000
Let's remind ourselves of the four or five important promises that we're making to these new scouts.

195
00:16:41,000 --> 00:16:46,000
We need to talk about how we're going to be ready to meet the challenge of fulfilling these promises for them.

196
00:16:46,000 --> 00:16:54,000
That will kind of center them on the idea that the new guys aren't bad, the new guys aren't a problem,

197
00:16:54,000 --> 00:17:00,000
but that the new guys are a welcome challenge that will help them grow as leaders

198
00:17:00,000 --> 00:17:03,000
and will help them become better scouts.

199
00:17:03,000 --> 00:17:05,000
Yeah, it's going to be great.

200
00:17:05,000 --> 00:17:09,000
The other thing to do, you know, make sure your adult leaders are prepared for the same thing.

201
00:17:09,000 --> 00:17:12,000
Hey, we have all these new guys. It's going to be great.

202
00:17:12,000 --> 00:17:17,000
Here's the specific promises of scouting that we need to fulfill for them.

203
00:17:17,000 --> 00:17:21,000
The Patrol Leaders Council is well underway on making this happen

204
00:17:21,000 --> 00:17:24,000
and we're going to need to be in a support role for it.

205
00:17:24,000 --> 00:17:28,000
You know we're going to have our challenges, but let's make sure that we keep our eye on the ball

206
00:17:28,000 --> 00:17:31,000
and on fulfilling the promises to these new guys.

207
00:17:31,000 --> 00:17:34,000
The other thing is is you're going to bring in a few new adult leaders,

208
00:17:34,000 --> 00:17:39,000
so you've got to get them into the culture of the troop and how you handle things

209
00:17:39,000 --> 00:17:45,000
and the way the boys lead, because I know- and I know that you know this too, Jason-

210
00:17:45,000 --> 00:17:50,000
you know your first couple of months are just going to seem like utter chaos

211
00:17:50,000 --> 00:17:56,000
and new leaders coming in off of being like a Weeblis den leader or something like that.

212
00:17:56,000 --> 00:17:59,000
they are not going to take the chaos very well.

213
00:17:59,000 --> 00:18:03,000
They're going to react to it in the way that they reacted to it when they were Weeblis den leaders

214
00:18:03,000 --> 00:18:06,000
and try and fix everything instead of standing back

215
00:18:06,000 --> 00:18:09,000
and letting the youth leaders take care of business.

216
00:18:09,000 --> 00:18:12,000
So you're going to have to explain that to them too.

217
00:18:12,000 --> 00:18:16,000
Now you know. if you've been at this for a while, you already know what I'm talking about,

218
00:18:16,000 --> 00:18:19,000
and it's going to cause a little chaos, it's going to cause a little difficulty,

219
00:18:19,000 --> 00:18:21,000
but what a wonderful challenge to have.

220
00:18:21,000 --> 00:18:26,000
I think that you guys are going to do just great if you stick with the idea

221
00:18:26,000 --> 00:18:29,000
of fulfilling the promises of scouting to these new scouts

222
00:18:29,000 --> 00:18:32,000
and you motivate your youth leaders to do that.

223
00:18:32,000 --> 00:18:36,000
As far as you know the nuts and bolts of it and how to make this happen

224
00:18:36,000 --> 00:18:40,000
and how to make that happen, I think that will come. as long as you're concentrating

225
00:18:40,000 --> 00:18:44,000
on those promises and fulfilling those promises

226
00:18:44,000 --> 00:18:47,000
and in making sure that every decision that the Patrol Leaders Council

227
00:18:47,000 --> 00:18:51,000
and your adult leaders make is focused on that.

228
00:18:51,000 --> 00:18:55,000
I think the final piece is to make sure that you have a really good

229
00:18:55,000 --> 00:19:02,000
parent's orientation set up, explaining exactly how things go from here on out,

230
00:19:02,000 --> 00:19:07,000
that they've left Weeblos, they've left Cub Scouts

231
00:19:07,000 --> 00:19:11,000
and now they're into this new phase and they're into Boy Scouts,

232
00:19:11,000 --> 00:19:14,000
and that's going to be quite a bit different,

233
00:19:14,000 --> 00:19:18,000
because the principal leader that they're going to talk to and ask questions of

234
00:19:18,000 --> 00:19:23,000
is the scout's patrol leader, and they need to know who that patrol leader is.

235
00:19:23,000 --> 00:19:26,000
they need to know how to be able to get in touch with him

236
00:19:26,000 --> 00:19:28,000
and how to get information.

237
00:19:28,000 --> 00:19:32,000
There'll also be, you know, many other pieces of practical advice

238
00:19:32,000 --> 00:19:36,000
that they'll need for camping trips and things.

239
00:19:36,000 --> 00:19:40,000
I think I've made a mistake in the past in not doing a very thorough job

240
00:19:40,000 --> 00:19:42,000
of orienting new parents around this,

241
00:19:42,000 --> 00:19:46,000
So I would suggest that if you don't have that kind of thing in place already,

242
00:19:46,000 --> 00:19:50,000
help it and get a real strong parent orientation thing going,

243
00:19:50,000 --> 00:19:52,000
especially with a group this large.

244
00:19:52,000 --> 00:19:56,000
So I would love to hear what happens in the future. Jason,

245
00:19:56,000 --> 00:20:00,000
I would love to hear the story and I really do appreciate you getting in touch

246
00:20:00,000 --> 00:20:02,000
and I hope that helps.

